<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Soviet Civil Administration</h1><p class="st-meta">1945 CE – 1948 CE · capital Pyongyang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Soviet occupation authority in northern Korea from 1945 to 1948, which brought Kim Il Sung to power and built the foundations of North Korea.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in August 1945, and its 25th Army under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-chistyakov">Ivan Chistyakov</a> swept into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. Under a hastily agreed line, Soviet forces occupied the peninsula north of the 38th parallel, and a civil administration was set up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> to govern it.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Soviet officers, overseen by Colonel General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terentii-shtykov">Terentii Shtykov</a>, ruled indirectly through local people's committees, sidelining nationalists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cho-man-sik">Cho Man-sik</a>. They promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-il-sung">Kim Il Sung</a>, a former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> officer, and backed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/provisional-peoples-committee-for-north-korea">Provisional People's Committee for North Korea</a>, which carried out land reform and nationalised industry. Japanese property and some industrial plant were seized.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Talks with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> on a unified government failed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">Democratic People's Republic of Korea</a> was proclaimed in September 1948 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-il-sung">Kim Il Sung</a> as premier, and Soviet troops withdrew by the end of that year.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/soviet-civil-administration.jpg" alt="Soviet Civil Administration" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: created by rotemliss from Image:Flag of the Soviet Union.svg. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Soviet_Union_(1936_%E2%80%93_1955).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1945 CE – 1948 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pyongyang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ivan Chistyakov, Terentii Shtykov</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Korean, Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/north-korea">North Korea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Soviet Civil Administration in Korea, SCA, Soviet occupation of North Korea</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1946&amp;focus=soviet-civil-administration" aria-label="Soviet Civil Administration on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7795 779 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Soviet Civil Administration lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8115 1035L8172 1082M8115 1035L5820 533M8115 1035L8115 1035"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8172 1082h0M5820 533h0M8115 1035h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8115 1035h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8115 1035h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Pyongyang. <a href="/atlas?year=1946&amp;focus=soviet-civil-administration">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
